Partial-response handling: for chunked streaming we recommend a client-side soft timeout per-chunk (e.g., 10s) and a larger request-level timeout (60s). If a chunk timeout fires, try one immediate retry of the missing chunk only; avoid restarting the whole stream unless the server closes the connection.

Node retry snippet (pseudo-code, integrate with your worker pool):

const backoff = (attempt) => Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, attempt), 30000) + Math.random() * 250;
async function callWithRetries(fn, maxAttempts=5) {
  for (let i = 0; i < maxAttempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      if (![429, 503].includes(err.status)) throw err;
      const wait = backoff(i);
      await sleep(wait);
    }
  }
  throw new Error('retry-failed');
}

Python example using requests (synchronous):
from requests import Session
from requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter
from urllib3.util.retry import Retry
s = Session()
retries = Retry(total=5, backoff_factor=0.5, status_forcelist=[429, 503], allowed_methods=['GET','POST'])
s.mount('https://', HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retries))

Operational recommendations:
- Start with conservative token-bucket limits on the client to avoid aggressive 429s during cutover (burst=200, refill=50/s as a starting point).
- For canary: route read (embedding) traffic first, then low-volume write/generation flows.
- Instrument at least request_id, model, token_count; we can generate a short alert set in Console to watch token and latency regressions.

